  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=(x-1)(x-3)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $\\{f(x)\\in\\mathbb{R}:1\\le x\\le2\\}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[-1,0]$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=(x-2)^2-1\\\\f(1)&=(1-1)(1-3)=0\\\\f(2)&=(2-1)(2-3)=-1\\end{align*}$$\nThe parabola decreases from $0$ to its minimum $-1$ as $x$ goes from $1$ to $2$. Both endpoints are included, so the required set is $[-1,0]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:0\\le f(x)\\le3\\}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[0,1]\\cup[3,4]$\n\nUse the upward-opening parabola to locate the inputs with outputs between $0$ and $3$, including the boundaries.",
          "worked": "The upward-opening parabola has zeros at $x=1$ and $x=3$, so $f(x)\\ge0$ when $x\\le1$ or $x\\ge3$. Find where its output is $3$:\n$$\\begin{align*}(x-1)(x-3)&=3\\\\x^2-4x+3&=3\\\\x(x-4)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }4\\end{align*}$$\nThe graph is on or below $y=3$ for $0\\le x\\le4$. Combining both restrictions gives $[0,1]\\cup[3,4]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
  },
